{"questions":[{"question_id":"1lg0xbm5d","paperTitle":"IIT-JEE 2007 Paper 1 Offline","year":2007,"examDate":2007,"subject":"chemistry","chapter":"electrochemistry","chapterGroup":"physical-chemistry","topic":"conductance-and-electrolysis","type":"mcq","marks":3,"negMarks":1,"permalink":"pif-the-cathode-is-a-hg-electrode-the-maximum-weight-g-jee-advanced-chemistry-some-basic-concepts-of-chemistry-i7byiabau4zvwp2y","content":"<p>If the cathode is a Hg electrode, the maximum weight (g) of amalgam formed from this solution is:</p>","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"200"},{"identifier":"B","content":"225"},{"identifier":"C","content":"400"},{"identifier":"D","content":"446"}],"correct_options":["D"],"explanation":"<p>Number of moles of Na<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.432ex\" height=\"1.754ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -775.2 633.1 775.2\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(33,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2B\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> = 2</p>\n<p>2NaCl = 2Na</p>\n<p>2Na + 2Hg <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.262ex\" height=\"1.181ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -511 1000 522\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> 2Na(Hg)</p>\n<p>By electrolysis we get 2 moles of sodium which can combine with exactly 2 moles of mercury to give 2 moles of amalgam.</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.509ex\" height=\"1.251ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -471 667 553\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2234\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2234\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> Maximum weight of Na amalgam (assuming equimolar Na and Hg) = 46 + 400 = 446 g</p>"}]}